  "response": "## Where the mix is\n\n`crimes context src/date.ts --format json` returns 6 findings; the one the task is about is `crime_00002` / `mixed_utc_local_methods` (\"Half-UTC, Half-Local\", severity high, confidence 0.85):\n\n```\n\"\\\"d\\\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41\"\n```\n\nOne correction on reading that evidence: L9 and L41 are **two different `d` variables** — one local to `todayKey()`, one local to `dateLabel()`. The detector buckets by identifier name at file scope, so it merged them. Checking the source resolves which one is actually mixed:\n\n- `todayKey()` (L7–10) — `getUTCFullYear()`, `getUTCMonth()`, `getUTCDate()`. Consistently UTC. Not the bug.\n- `dateLabel()` (L39–42) — **this is the mixed one.**\n\n## The function to fix first: `dateLabel()`\n\n**Receiver whose methods disagree: `d`, declared at `src/date.ts:40`.**\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  const d = new Date();\n  return d.getUTCFullYear() + \"-\" + d.getMonth() + \"-\" + d.getDate();\n}\n```\n\n`getUTCFullYear()` (UTC family) against `getMonth()` and `getDate()` (local family), on the same receiver, in the same expression.\n\n**Concrete failure.** Host at `America/New_York`, instant `2027-01-01T02:00Z`. UTC year is 2027; local wall clock is still 2026-12-31. The year comes from one calendar, the month and day from another: `\"2027-11-31\"`. The `11` is December read zero-indexed (`date_string_concat`, `crime_00005`, is on this same line). So for a five-hour window every New Year the label names a year, month, and day that never co-occurred, and every other day of the year it's off by one month. Two consumers computing \"today\" — one via `todayKey()`, one via `dateLabel()` — already disagree on the same instant, before the timezone bug enters.\n\nPick UTC. `todayKey()` is already all-UTC, and both functions produce a machine-readable `YYYY-MM-DD` key rather than anything a user reads, so UTC is the family the file is already trying to use.\n\n## Proposed refactor\n\nFormat through one boundary helper instead of assembling fragments — that eliminates the family mix, the zero-index month, and the missing zero-padding in one move:\n\n```ts\n/** UTC calendar day as YYYY-MM-DD. Storage/keying format — not for display. */\nexport function dateLabel(now: Date = new Date()): string {\n  return now.toISOString().slice(0, 10);\n}\n\nexport function todayKey(now: Date = new Date()): string {\n  return dateLabel(now);\n}\n```\n\nThe optional `now` parameter is the smallest step toward `crime_00001` (`direct_date`, 8 clock reads in this file) — it keeps every existing call site working while making the function testable.\n\nIf some caller genuinely needs a *local* label, that's a separate function at the display boundary, with the zone and locale named explicitly rather than inherited from the host — which also fixes `locale_drift` at L46:\n\n```ts\nconst DAY_LABEL = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-CA\", {\n  timeZone: \"America/New_York\", // or a value threaded from config\n  year: \"numeric\", month: \"2-digit\", day: \"2-digit\",\n});\nexport function localDateLabel(d: Date): string {\n  return DAY_LABEL.format(d);\n}\n```\n\n**Behaviour change to confirm before merging.** Output shape changes: `\"2026-1-5\"` → `\"2026-01-05\"`, and the month becomes 1-indexed. That's a fix, but it's a fix to a string other code may be parsing or persisting. `dateLabel` and `todayKey` have no in-repo callers (`grep` over `src/` finds only the declarations), but both are `export`ed, so external consumers and any stored keys are out of the compiler's reach. If day-keys are already persisted, the old and new formats won't match — that needs a migration or a read-side normaliser, not just this edit.\n\nRemaining date findings in the file, not addressed here: `timezone_unsafe_parse` (L29, L33 — zoneless literals) and `dst_naive_arithmetic` (L52 — `+ 86400000`).",
